{
  "dom_id": "step-q85073",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "A triangle $D$ has vertices $(-2,-1),(3,-1),(1,3).$ What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's sketch our region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/assets/10c831c616279f443d10ed478a8b3b173d598da6e9b0b45edb7ecf487cf97f92.png)\n\nRecall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 4=10.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=2\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 0=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|\\,6\\,|\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
